Z||000006||FieroLisa||09-07-2001||10:33 PM||||Whew I'm back!!!!!!<P>Stupid stupid stupid car.... the last toll booth right around Charleston I felt a little too much brake pedal travel, pulled into a gas station, there was brake fluid everywhere on the drivers side rear.  Resevoir was about empty. Filled up with some dot 3 so i could get some pressure on the pedal and didn't touch the brakes for the next 200 miles- lots of downshifting. Wasn't bad till 10 miles from home there was some serious stop and go traffic. I didnt quite have the "stopping" part down.<BR>But I'm here and hey I'll just take this as a chance to learn how to re-do my brakes- I'm flushing and redoing everything- brake fluid is sludge-y cause of too much water in there, I guess.<P>UG!!!!! But I'm finally back in Ohio, yay!<BR><P>------------------<BR>    <IMG SRC="http://www.webjolt.f2s.com/pictures/fierolisa.gif">     <BR><A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/fierolisa/" TARGET=_blank>FieroLisa's Website</A>*<A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/ncfieros/" TARGET=_blank>NC Tarheels Fieros Site</A><P>AIM: FieroLisa||||reg||1
Z||000007||Fformula88||09-07-2001||10:41 PM||||Glad to hear you made it back ok Lisa!<P>It may also be a good idea for everyone to replace the brake fluid, even if you won't be replacing the lines.  I am sure a lot of the people here know this, but for those who don't... moisture in your brake fluid will rust the lines out from the inside.  My uncle (a good mechanic!) told me once that when the fluid gets that brownish color, that means there is moisture in it.  The darker, the more moisture.  <P>I replaced my fluid a last spring when I rebuilt the rear calipers.  It wasn't too hard.  After I bled the brakes, I kept opening one valve a time on each caliper and pumping fluid thru it until it came out clear on all four wheels!Just remember to keep the resevoir filled while your doing this or you will get air in them and need to bleed them again!||||reg||1
Z||000008||Leadfoot||09-07-2001||11:23 PM||||Yup! That rear brake line goes when you least expect it.  Glad to hear she is all right.||||reg||1
Z||000009||ray b||09-07-2001||11:58 PM||||I wish I could say if this ever happens again<BR>but just know it will CRIMP the steel line to prevent fluid loss in the bad rubber line yes now you must replace the steel line but at least you live to do it.<P>------------------<BR>Question wonder and be wierd||||reg||1
Z||000010||Formula88||09-08-2001||01:49 AM||||Glad to hear you made it ok, Lisa.  Now, if you were still in NC, I'd offer my services to help with your car, but since you SO far away.....   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>||||reg||14
Z||000011||Fierowrecker||09-08-2001||01:52 AM||||Glad to hear you are home safe and sound!<P>Now, why do we have dual master cylinder, and seperate front/rear system, when the propotioning valve negates the benefits???<BR>It would be much safer if we had true dual system, so if we have a failure, the fronts or backs would stay working...<BR>Even my Ford F-250 has the same problem...<BR>crash...||||reg||1
Z||000012||artherd||09-08-2001||05:22 AM||||Hey Lisa, Glad to hear you're OK!<P>Just remenbered something about you using DOT5 Silicone fluid in your car? (can't remember for sure if it was you however)    Anyway, I wonder if this in some way had to do with the failure? (dosen't matter though, the rubber lines are infamous for this in any case  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif">  Did you infact brake a rubber line, or the hard steel?<P>Hrm, now where's that darned "Work Right" button?  I know they hid one somewhere on the Fiero, if we could only FIND the (&@#$* thing...  !  <P>Best!<BR>Ben.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/88ttop.jpg">  <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top Metalic Red<BR>88 Formula, Silver<BR>87 Coupe, Metalic Red<BR>"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>                -Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||3
